#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3d5ffe46-c0ac-5309-a31b-28a6a1d927e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9054" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff904851d01e984d3b1719623d6e4aa4fbde9f070cb4e3fa4121f96a55a34bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/c7/c7bc0b1331127f20b8121b32111c152029c75bd0c6b7ebb0abab5425445285f1.png)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/a1/a1d998bfdf9ba31174396de548e12e0fae07fdbfa5c2a0e62b0c90ba8071ba64.png)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/bc/bcf76f04db85e4207c2b26e2a7848812c631330f3d65129e93430df1e71592f6.png)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/17/17eb9f4a4768401d60cdd40aad65bb184799d5c718e1ff97a2204f48a0a8b46b.png)\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "30daf182-0ed6-5ddd-994e-a5af7c6c6c01" :question/math-academy-id "e-9056" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-5I$ on the nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w},\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v}_n$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}2$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank ${\\color{red}2},$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+{\\color{blue}3}I$ maps $\\mathbf{u}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}3$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}3$   corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-3}$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} \\xrightarrow{A-{\\color{black}5}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-{\\color{black}5}I$ maps $\\mathbf{y}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}1$ step, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}1,$ not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "79ffef9f-8be0-581f-b2f1-dc796d2cf94b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9057" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1a51fd3bae168f0b61e564cc421b2258c70351684c0480538b09ead5763aacd.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\n- First, we compute $A-I{:}$\n\n![](images/d0/d07685a00b1e304d31fdf1b2c4067d8bf521023c448c2ccae8e918d269159ec6.png)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/ff/ff5818e172da1edc5b12e07f16b232964cc9b051a22498949119f5ae536e3ef8.png)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/f7/f7450b8ba8ba29d97b413cb50d429cfb1a9aa534d5908e1917b426068cbb1bdf.png)\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/27/27c1a886c0b7cb4c4dfa427de1dcb44da5a98d70c24dd80a877d62f7ff9114e5.png)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/1a/1a9cac337deaeaffeafb270281404e3a1917abcf85914fd8b737df8d89a3af01.png)\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a9e8175-eaf4-56fa-bb5a-51cbe3657e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148818} {:answer/id #uuid "b78056bf-024a-5970-8360-52255d2e3bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148819} {:answer/id #uuid "e5168ab7-fc1b-5237-8ca1-624180e8edcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148820} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d5a51b-7efc-5866-872b-d03ff13bf073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148821} {:answer/id #uuid "32fd699a-3e56-5732-b2ce-5b6965910811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148822}] :answer-field/id #uuid "169702d2-e217-532a-8070-256f03e04ad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148817} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b9c8ea-3433-5e98-ae2b-66d5074675b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362656} {:answer/id #uuid "d30126e1-6458-5988-a441-53d4d29bf115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362657} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9f5762-5d97-545d-8ca2-b371d5cdf90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362658} {:answer/id #uuid "16e7f43b-5245-5b84-8f13-c4e0178a0177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362659} {:answer/id #uuid "b4447d56-1641-5e1f-ac40-5127d0aecf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b9c8ea-3433-5e98-ae2b-66d5074675b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362656} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9c9380-d4e5-5d77-bc33-cb4ca372f9b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7971d731-7317-51d2-bfe1-46fc4b23c3f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109117" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+3I:$\n\n$$\nA+3I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22442fa5-3ac2-5b6d-8550-6ad60d10e199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241477} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb021b2-1015-50c9-82f0-4e3db31fe829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241864} {:answer/id #uuid "664f1704-9bf7-5ed2-bdcd-e673e693ceac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242104} {:answer/id #uuid "67b18cc0-15fc-5a64-b685-24c74f1d28a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242111} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a32f11-c203-592f-95cc-5eba4cbdfb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "664f1704-9bf7-5ed2-bdcd-e673e693ceac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242104} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b988a7-f80c-506d-a67f-2ddc646b8c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cf2c42d-88e3-50f2-bb3a-16ab087d4389" :question/math-academy-id "q-109236" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I=A-1I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d2b9d26-8293-5a06-81bf-a5d8768ab1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241725} {:answer/id #uuid "4e059d3f-50a9-5d66-bbba-0d70399855ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241881} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd4d0aa-ccaf-528d-a95d-b920e6aa13a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242177} {:answer/id #uuid "76f397bc-53a2-5ba3-b32c-891ac2f4b5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242234} {:answer/id #uuid "9de02649-0cd7-593b-a2c5-9ca8945e60f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fd4d0aa-ccaf-528d-a95d-b920e6aa13a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242177} :answer-field/id #uuid "417546af-5e04-5c33-856e-84ad4da01a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a61bdaa-5171-5017-8ae8-0bac73d29cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109244" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-6I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e9efe6-968d-54c9-ba7f-d8bfc0344af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241358} {:answer/id #uuid "20dac993-1771-57da-aea4-5a53bae88d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241464} {:answer/id #uuid "971f6555-302a-5456-818a-0917a567ea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242505} {:answer/id #uuid "b7db1f32-24e0-5572-949c-0c739c96c41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242791} {:answer/id #uuid "d20d30f0-c9d8-57d3-8218-55ec4117f2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20dac993-1771-57da-aea4-5a53bae88d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241464} :answer-field/id #uuid "04917658-3d46-5d2e-85f2-f45f68c5a185" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65e6b2ee-a472-5db0-bf96-384d33845a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109360" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+9I$ and $A-I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-9$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $z$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n and $v\\ne 0,$ \ni.e. $A+9I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is  false. Since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, since \n\n$$\nz\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-1I$ maps $z$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3528dc7a-edc0-54b3-bc5e-f088015cc468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241644} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8d0b0d-10a8-5050-9ccf-f84b7ae89c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241701} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc42dce-cb33-5ebd-81ab-a940ad3e8eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242283} {:answer/id #uuid "9efcde9e-50cc-58ad-9453-2e8c5b64cabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242575} {:answer/id #uuid "a430d0ea-44d9-550f-8830-1b2b2a892158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3528dc7a-edc0-54b3-bc5e-f088015cc468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241644} :answer-field/id #uuid "16edc7c5-1547-5425-97d2-8194abf4981c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dab8a46d-1b84-5b26-b9d0-b9d19dd8bea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109440" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+4I$ and $A+3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d3a269-f7df-5be8-8c0a-c58771bf3313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241676} {:answer/id #uuid "869c2a77-c110-5a9e-8730-019b98ad1d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242360} {:answer/id #uuid "9e265f89-9d57-5ba5-951b-b7eabd9027cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242565} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5e9232-a129-548e-b486-ec287dd87153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242988} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a3256d-e444-52f1-9bf1-ed1666c753df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a3256d-e444-52f1-9bf1-ed1666c753df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243361} :answer-field/id #uuid "f900ef13-9248-52f7-8b67-b6ec2ce93fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f8f24d7-923f-567b-b949-576aeecc58aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-109506" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine out statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-3I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131723b1-04e0-5afb-b47f-b4d05d1df63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244490} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd2eb1f-1d6e-59fb-9827-b17359aaf4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245131} {:answer/id #uuid "77bdf862-1837-5e8b-a68a-3e5fa2a3385d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245375} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2ca2da-7622-54e5-a685-fcc2f8d40a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245841} {:answer/id #uuid "aee5383f-bf90-5f85-aadc-05f837be2ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aee5383f-bf90-5f85-aadc-05f837be2ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245873} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dae667a-3a97-5762-926d-1ea73a981d60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c9bad97-e078-5fd9-8cb8-6324032501fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109508" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+4I:$\n\n$$\nA+4I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362684} {:answer/id #uuid "fea449ab-857f-5e97-a2dd-9a5decfbfe3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362685} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e9c614-fb32-5fc3-a8e1-80768c7a6164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362686} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8fd250-9f94-565b-afb8-d6406e6e4d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362687} {:answer/id #uuid "e4559c11-53ee-582b-ac61-017f9bf594ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362684} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc073027-8d63-5f72-bb4e-ddd774541c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c9089c-246d-51fb-b493-4538acc22376" :question/math-academy-id "q-109574" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18659916-aaf4-573c-9a3b-15946350b712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244532} {:answer/id #uuid "67dbddfd-c8a6-564d-9232-654bc7de88dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245233} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa71e62-3863-5892-9c8e-d7142e9daffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245679} {:answer/id #uuid "c91285aa-bc48-586c-9a68-18d06c294a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246097} {:answer/id #uuid "d5810813-5efa-576b-8341-d670f60431c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67dbddfd-c8a6-564d-9232-654bc7de88dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245233} :answer-field/id #uuid "7915b691-2bc7-579a-91ec-6f307f69d4c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd32b406-fe2e-5167-ace1-4082f15020f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-109585" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c26e67-c3dc-5457-b6e3-77c851c0563e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244395} {:answer/id #uuid "86ea91a4-0172-570f-a092-67a3172cc408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245501} {:answer/id #uuid "8c01cea2-3a2e-55ed-934c-7393ce2b8c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245541} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8608d9-2139-58da-9594-cdc1a336f36f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245724} {:answer/id #uuid "a41e13bf-5794-5d2c-bb9c-a67f9ff83aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41e13bf-5794-5d2c-bb9c-a67f9ff83aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245779} :answer-field/id #uuid "82ade937-dfe0-5ac2-9cc4-812779563b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f4f929d-c8e6-554e-bc37-364f30077a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109587" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+5I:$\n\n$$\nA+5I=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
